stead of developing a separate notation for upside down fences, we will allow the first part of the composition to be zero in those instances.\n\nA sequence is called unimodal if there exists an index m such that It was conjectured in [8] that the rank sequence of is unimodal. A more specific conjecture about the behavior of the coefficients was given in [7]. A sequence is called top interlacing if where is the ceiling function. Similarly, the sequence is bottom interlacing if with being the floor function. Note that top interlacing, as well as bottom interlacing sequences are unimodal. We further call a polynomial bottom interlacing (resp. top interlacing), if its coefficient sequence has that property.\n\nTo prove Theorem 1.3, we will define a circular version of the fence poset, where the first and last node are related.\n\n## 3. Circular fences\n\nFor a composition of n we define the circular fence poset of α, denoted to be the fence poset of α with the additional relation , so that we end up with a circular poset with n nodes. We will denote the corresponding order ideal lattice, rank polynomial, and rank sequence by and respectively. We will call the nodes that correspond to {\\alpha }_{i} the ith segment of .\n\nCircular fences have substantial intrinsic symmetry. Shifting the parts cyclically by two steps gives the same object and reversing the order of the parts preserves the rank sequence. In the special case when all the segments are of size 1, the object we obtain is called a crown. Crowns were previously studied in [11] where it was shown that the corresponding rank polynomials are always symmetric, and they are unimodal when the number of segments is different than 4. Examining the one step shift allows us to directly say that the symmetry holds when one of the segments is larger as well. This will serve as the basis to prove that in fact, for any circular fence poset we get a rank symmetric lattice.\n\nLemma 3.1\n\nShifting the parts of α cyclically by one step reverses the rank sequence . In particular where the number of segments is even is symmetric for any .\n\nProof\n\nThis follows as a cyclic shift of one step on a circular fence is equivalent to reversing the order relation or flipping the poset upside down. Making a cyclic shift of one step followed by reversing the parts of gives which has the exact same structure but a reversed rank sequence. □\n\nIn general, rank polynomials for circular fences are no easier to calculate than their noncircular counterparts and we only have formulas for a limited number of cases. The case when was considered in [10]. They were able to formulate the rank polynomial in terms of Chebyshev polynomials of the first kind, defined recursively by , and .\n\nProposition 3.2\n\n## [10]\n\nWe have where 2s is the number of segments of .\n\nNote that when , we get the polynomial , which is not unimodal.\n\nSome other small cases that can be easily calculated by hand are listed in Table 1 below.\n\nTable 1. Ideal count and rank polynomial for small cases.\n\nα  | Ideal Count  | Rank Polynomial\n--- | --- | ---\n(a,b)  | ab + 2  | 1 + q[a]_{q}[b]_{q} + q^{a+b}\n(a,1,b,1)  | ab + 2a + 2b + 2  | [a+2]_{q}[b+2]_{q} − q^{a+1} − q^{b+1}\n(a,b,c,d)  | abcd + ab + cd + ad + bc + 2  | 1 + q[a]_{q}[d]_{q} + q[b]_{q}[c]_{q} + q^{a+b+1}[c]_{q}[d]_{q} + q^{c+d+1}[a]_{q}[b]_{q} + q^{a+b+c+d}\n(a,a,a,a)  | a^{4} + 4a^{2} + 2  | \n\nThe cases of and are indeed quite straightforward. The lattice formed by the ideals of is formed by the direct product of two chains of lengths a and b, with an added minimum element (for the empty ideal) and maximum element (for the full ideal): . Here, the position on {C}_{a} corresponds to the number of unshared elements in the segment of size a, whereas the position on {C}_{b} describes the number of unshared elements in the segment of size b. The natural symmetric chain decomposition on can easily be extended to accommodate the two added nodes, as seen in Fig. 3 f